# Shell scripts and live-build files MUST keep LF endings —
# a hook with CRLF endings breaks inside the Debian chroot ("/bin/sh^M").
* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf
*.chroot text eol=lf
# *.chroot above catches *.hook.chroot and *.list.chroot but NOT *.hook.binary,
# and nothing else in this file caught it either — `git check-attr text eol`
# reported 0950-boot-branding.hook.binary as text:auto, eol:unspecified. A FRESH
# clone with core.autocrlf=true (which is what this machine is set to) therefore
# writes it CRLF, and build.ps1 mounts the working tree straight into the build
# container. Caught before a clone did it rather than after, like the Konsole
# rule below.
#
# The hook is a #!/bin/sh script live-build execs on the assembled ISO tree, so
# a CR on the shebang makes it "/bin/sh^M" — and a binary hook live-build
# declines to run fails in SILENCE, the failure build.sh's chmod comment already
# names: the ISO simply comes out with Debian's generic boot menu, no
# free-vs-Pro title and no "with screen reader" entry, and nothing in the log
# says so. Matching the DIRECTORY rather than the extension so the next hook of
# any kind is covered too; the *.png rule at the bottom still wins for any
# artwork that lands in here.
config/hooks/** text eol=lf
auto/* text eol=lf
*.desc text eol=lf
*.qml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
packages/**/DEBIAN/* text eol=lf
config/includes.chroot/usr/bin/* text eol=lf
config/includes.chroot/usr/lib/live/config/* text eol=lf
# usr/lib/dagric holds extensionless shell scripts (display-autoscale), which
# *.sh does not match. Without this line they fall through to `* text=auto` and
# a checkout with core.autocrlf=true writes them CRLF — build.ps1 mounts the
# working tree straight into the build container, so that ships as "/bin/sh^M".
config/includes.chroot/usr/lib/dagric/* text eol=lf
# The offline manual and the guide/welcome pages: browsers do not care about
# CRLF, but dagric-manual greps and rewrites these files, so keep them uniform.
config/includes.chroot/usr/share/dagric/** text eol=lf
# systemd drop-ins, sysctl.d, udev rules, /etc/default — same chroot, same rule
config/includes.chroot/etc/** text eol=lf
# Linux-side config that CRLF would break (KConfig, .desktop, skel dotfiles)
config/includes.chroot/etc/skel/** text eol=lf
*.desktop text eol=lf
# The boot loaders' own config, which live-build copies over its templates and
# which then ships INSIDE the ISO. isolinux/stdmenu.cfg was falling through to
# `* text=auto`, so a clone with core.autocrlf=true wrote it CRLF — and
# build.ps1 mounts the working tree straight into the build container. Same
# class of bug as the usr/lib/dagric line above; the PNG rule at the bottom
# still claims any artwork that lands in here.
config/bootloaders/** text eol=lf
# Plasma colour schemes are KConfig INI files and were falling through to
# `* text=auto`, so a clone with core.autocrlf=true wrote CRLF into them —
# and build.ps1 mounts the working tree straight into the build container.
# This has always applied to DagricLight/DagricDark, not just the newer
# high-contrast scheme; same class of bug as the usr/lib/dagric line above.
config/includes.chroot/usr/share/color-schemes/** text eol=lf
# Konsole's scheme and profile are KConfig INI files too, and they were falling
# through to `* text=auto` in exactly the same way — git announced it while they
# were being committed ("LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches
# it"). Caught before a Windows clone could write them CRLF rather than after.
# The failure would have been the quiet kind this file keeps warning about:
# Konsole does not report a malformed profile, it drops it with a debug-only
# warning and opens stock Breeze, so the terminal would simply have looked
# unbranded with nothing anywhere saying why.
config/includes.chroot/usr/share/konsole/** text eol=lf
# The boot splash and the login theme, which were falling through to
# `* text=auto` in exactly the same way as the four rules around this one.
#
# dagric.plymouth is the file that names the module and points at the script. A
# CRLF checkout makes every value carry a trailing CR, so ModuleName becomes
# "script\r" — no plugin of that name exists — and ScriptFile points at a path
# that does not. plymouthd does not report a broken theme; it falls back, and on
# a machine where the fallback is not present the boot screen is simply black.
# That is the worst failure this repository keeps finding: silent, at boot,
# on the first thing a new owner sees.
#
# sddm/themes/**/theme.conf and breeze/theme.conf.user are KConfig-style INI and
# behave like the Konsole files above. The PNG and default.face.icon rules in the
# binary block below still claim the artwork inside these directories.
config/includes.chroot/usr/share/plymouth/** text eol=lf
config/includes.chroot/usr/share/sddm/** text eol=lf
# LibreOffice's registry .xcd. Lower stakes than the KConfig files above — XML
# normalises line endings, so CRLF here would very likely parse — but "very
# likely" is not a standard worth applying to the file that decides whether the
# office suite starts, and git announced the conversion while it was being
# committed. Same one-line fix as the three rules above it.
config/includes.chroot/usr/lib/libreoffice/** text eol=lf
# systemd units shipped outside /etc. The etc/** rule above already covers the
# drop-ins; dagric-ssh-hostkeys.service was the first unit to live under
# /usr/lib/systemd, and git announced the conversion while it was being
# committed — the same tell as the Konsole and LibreOffice rules above.
#
# Same "very likely fine" as the .xcd, and the same answer. systemd counts \r as
# whitespace and strips it off the end of a value, so a CRLF unit would almost
# certainly parse. But this particular unit is the one that gives each machine
# its own SSH host keys, and if a stray \r ever did land inside a
# ConditionPathExists the condition would simply never match: no keys, sshd
# refusing to start, and not one line anywhere saying why. One rule is cheaper
# than depending on a whitespace-stripping detail staying true.
config/includes.chroot/usr/lib/systemd/** text eol=lf
# The Calamares helper scripts, and this one is not hypothetical either — it was
# caught by running `git check-attr` on a file the moment it was written, which
# reported text:auto and eol:unspecified. None of the rules above reach
# usr/share/calamares.
#
# The file is Dagric's replacement for calamares-bootloader-config, a
# #!/bin/bash script Calamares executes DURING AN INSTALL. A fresh clone with
# core.autocrlf=true — which is what this machine is set to — would write it
# CRLF, the shebang would become "/bin/bash^M", and the install would die with
# "External command finished with errors" at the bootloader step. That is
# character-for-character the same dialog as the bug the replacement exists to
# fix, so it would have been diagnosed twice and blamed on the wrong thing,
# after the disk had already been repartitioned.
#
# Same class as the *.hook.binary rule further up. Matching the DIRECTORY so any
# future helper is covered without anyone having to remember this.
config/includes.chroot/usr/share/calamares/** text eol=lf
# The gettext SOURCE catalogues. Read by msgmerge/msgfmt on a build host that
# may be Windows; CRLF in a .po is tolerated but produces a pointless diff on
# every round trip. (The compiled .mo files are binary — see the bottom.)
po/*.po text eol=lf
# The template was left on text=auto by the rule above, which only matches
# *.po — `git check-attr` reported po/dagric.pot as eol:unspecified, so a fresh
# clone with autocrlf=true writes it CRLF. Same omission as the *.hook.binary
# one further up, and the same fix. It does not reach the image (the .pot is
# never compiled or shipped), but tools/i18n-ci-check.sh compares a regenerated
# .pot against this one, and on a Windows checkout every line would differ.
po/*.pot text eol=lf
# The build-host python tools. NOT a live bug and it should be recorded as such:
# build.sh and container-build.sh both invoke these as `python3 tools/foo.py`,
# so the shebang is never used and CRLF would be harmless — python reads mixed
# line endings fine. The rule is here for the day somebody runs ./tools/foo.py
# directly on a Linux box after a Windows clone and gets
# "/usr/bin/env: 'python3\r': No such file or directory", which reads like a
# broken python install rather than a checkout artefact. The one python file
# that SHIPS is already covered by the usr/share/dagric/** rule above.
tools/*.py text eol=lf
# The website. Lowest stakes on this list and recorded as such: browsers do not
# care about CRLF, and these files are served as-is rather than parsed by
# anything of ours. The rule is here because git announced the conversion on
# faq.html and features.html while an edit to the AppArmor wording was being
# committed, and because SHA256SUMS three lines down already has to be byte-exact
# — a tree where some of site/ is normalised and the rest is not is the state
# that produces a diff full of files nobody touched. The binary rules at the
# bottom still claim the artwork and the signatures inside this directory.
site/** text eol=lf

# Windows scripts keep CRLF
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries. These come LAST on purpose: gitattributes lets a later line win,
# and the directory rules above (etc/**, usr/share/dagric/**) would otherwise
# claim the PNGs sitting inside those trees.
*.png binary
*.iso binary
# The site/** rule above declares the whole website `text eol=lf`, and it would
# have claimed these — a binary silently rewritten by the text filter, which is
# the exact trap the .pf2 note below describes. The PNGs in site/assets were
# already safe because *.png sits right here; the promo video, the WebP images
# and the favicon had nothing covering them. Caught by listing every non-HTML
# file under site/ and asking git what attribute it had, rather than by
# assuming the new rule only touched pages.
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.mp4 binary
# .jpg was the gap this list left, and it bit exactly as the note above
# predicts. site/assets/walkthrough-poster.jpg — the poster frame for the
# install walkthrough — committed one byte SHORT of the file on disk, because
# `site/** text eol=lf` claimed it and the filter stripped the CR out of the
# single CRLF pair that happened to occur in the JPEG's entropy-coded data.
# Caught by `git add` printing "CRLF will be replaced by LF" on a photograph,
# then confirming with `git cat-file -p` against the working file: 84401 bytes
# committed, 84402 on disk. Nothing warns you twice — a corrupted poster looks
# fine to whoever committed it, because their working copy is untouched, and
# breaks only for the next person to clone.
# .jpeg is here for the same reason .jpg is: the extension a file happens to
# be given must not decide whether git rewrites its bytes.
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
# GRUB's bitmap fonts. They live under config/includes.chroot/usr/share/dagric/,
# which the directory rule above declares `text eol=lf` — so without this line
# git treats a binary as text and rewrites its bytes on the way in.
#
# This has not corrupted anything yet and that is luck, not design. Git's text
# filter converts CRLF pairs, and today's three .pf2 files happen to contain
# bare CR bytes (51, 54 and 66 of them) without a single LF following one. The
# next regeneration by config/bootloaders/make-boot-art.sh — a different glyph
# range, a different size — puts a 0x0D next to a 0x0A and the committed file is
# silently wrong from then on. The failure mode is the worst kind: GRUB does not
# report a broken font, it quietly falls back to 16px Unifont and the menu just
# looks like the theme was ignored.
*.pf2 binary
# *.rgba was here for usr/share/dagric/icon/dagric-64.rgba, a raw pixel blob fed
# to a python helper that replaced the wizard's _NET_WM_ICON from outside. Both
# are gone: the title-bar icon turned out to resolve through the window's
# app_id and a desktop file, which needs no pixels of our own. The rule is kept
# because the reasoning still applies to any raw-pixel file added later, and
# because a binary silently rewritten on checkout is the kind of fault nobody
# finds by reading a diff.
*.rgba binary
# Compiled gettext catalogues. Git's NUL-byte heuristic already detects these
# as binary, so this is insurance rather than a live bug — but a .mo checked
# out with CRLF conversion is a CORRUPT catalogue, and the failure mode is a
# language that silently reverts to English with nothing in any log.
*.mo binary
# SDDM's default avatar is a PNG that must not be named .png — SDDM looks for
# this exact filename. `* text=auto` would guess right today, but a file whose
# name says nothing about its contents should not depend on a guess.
config/includes.chroot/usr/share/sddm/faces/default.face.icon binary

# Verification artifacts must stay byte-exact (CRLF breaks sha256sum -c / gpg)
site/SHA256SUMS text eol=lf
site/SHA256SUMS.sig -text
site/dagric-signing-key.asc -text

# The dearmored repo keyring apt verifies against. Binary; the text filter
# would rewrite its bytes and apt would reject every signature it makes.
*.gpg binary

# COMPILED TRANSLATION CATALOGUES. Exactly the same trap as the .jpg above, and
# it was already armed: the eleven Qt catalogues added for the login screen and
# the installer slideshow sit under directory rules that declare them
# `text eol=lf`, so `git check-attr` reported text:set on every one. A .qm is a
# binary blob with a magic header and byte offsets — collapse one CRLF pair
# inside it and QTranslator::load() refuses the file, which reverts the login
# screen and the installer to English in five languages with nothing in any log.
#
# Not corrupt today only because no current .qm happens to contain a 0x0D 0x0A
# pair. That is luck, and it expires the next time anyone regenerates them.
#
# The gettext .mo files already have their own rule further up (line ~207) and
# were never at risk. The .qm had nothing, which is the whole point: relying on
# a directory rule to protect a binary format is what left them exposed.
*.qm binary
